In computer networking, "binding" means attaching a network service or program (like Burp Suite) to a specific IP address and port number on your machine.
Think of your computer as a large apartment building:
• The IP addresses are the different entrances to the building (e.g., the front door, the back door, or the internal maintenance hallway).
• The Port number is a specific apartment room number inside that building (e.g., Room 8082).
When you configure Burp Suite's listener, you are telling the computer exactly which "entrance" it is allowed to open to let traffic into Room 8082:

1. Loopback only (127.0.0.1)

• The Analogy: This is like locking the front and back doors and only allowing people already inside the apartment hallway to knock on Room 8082.
• What it means: Only programs running on that exact same computer (like your local browser) can talk to Burp Suite. No outside device can touch it.

2. Specific Address (e.g., 192.168.1.105)

• The Analogy: This is like unlocking only the front door of the building.
• What it means: Burp Suite will only listen for traffic coming through that specific network card (like your Wi-Fi network). Your emulator can connect to it, but if you connect to a VPN later, Burp won't listen to that new VPN entrance.

3. All Interfaces (0.0.0.0)

• The Analogy: This unlocks every single door to the building at the same time—the front door, the back door, the garage, and the internal hallways.
• What it means: Burp Suite listens everywhere. Your emulator can talk to it, devices on your local Wi-Fi can talk to it, and if you connect to a VPN or a hotspot, those can talk to it too.

Why it matters for your setup:

Because your Android Emulator acts like a completely separate physical phone plugged into a virtual router, it cannot see the internal maintenance hallway (127.0.0.1). It has to come through the "front door" (your local Ubuntu network IP). By binding to "All Interfaces", you guarantee that no matter how the emulator tries to route its traffic to your computer, Burp Suite will be listening at that door to catch it.
